WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.106
MCP security protects the full integration path between AI applications, users, tools,

00:00:05.106 --> 00:00:08.027
resources, servers, identities, secrets, and downstream systems.

00:00:08.027 --> 00:00:13.033
The security boundary changes because AI-assisted behavior can discover resources, call tools,

00:00:13.033 --> 00:00:14.702
and influence real systems.

00:00:14.702 --> 00:00:18.456
A model answer is no longer just a paragraph;

00:00:18.456 --> 00:00:24.297
it may be connected to actions and data flows that require application security, identity,

00:00:24.297 --> 00:00:25.966
operations, and governance controls.

00:00:25.966 --> 00:00:30.137
Strong MCP security starts with architecture visibility and trust boundaries.

00:00:30.137 --> 00:00:34.309
Teams should map data flow, authority flow, and action flow.

00:00:34.309 --> 00:00:38.481
They should identify hosts, clients, servers, tools, resources, prompts, users,

00:00:38.481 --> 00:00:42.653
service identities, secrets, local files, cloud services, and downstream APIs.

00:00:42.653 --> 00:00:48.494
That map gives the organization a shared view of what the agent can read,

00:00:48.494 --> 00:00:52.248
what it can call, and what it can change.

00:00:52.248 --> 00:00:56.003
Prompt wording alone is not a reliable security boundary.

00:00:56.003 --> 00:00:59.341
Authorization, least privilege, consent, tool allowlisting, server provenance,

00:00:59.341 --> 00:01:03.095
argument validation, resource filtering, tenant isolation, approval gates, logging,

00:01:03.095 --> 00:01:06.433
and monitoring must be enforced outside the model.

00:01:06.433 --> 00:01:11.022
Tool descriptions and external content should be treated as security-relevant inputs,

00:01:11.022 --> 00:01:12.691
not automatically trusted instructions.

00:01:12.691 --> 00:01:19.366
Data minimization and secrets handling help prevent sensitive information from entering places it does not belong.

00:01:19.366 --> 00:01:21.452
The goal is controlled adoption.

00:01:21.452 --> 00:01:26.875
MCP can make AI applications more useful, but useful systems need governance, recoverability,

00:01:26.875 --> 00:01:28.127
and incident response.

00:01:28.127 --> 00:01:31.464
Treat tools like integrations, servers like supply-chain dependencies,

00:01:31.464 --> 00:01:33.133
resources like governed data,

00:01:33.133 --> 00:01:36.888
and model-generated actions like requests that require policy enforcement.

00:01:36.888 --> 00:01:39.391
Done well, MCP becomes auditable, privacy-aware,

00:01:39.391 --> 00:01:44.397
and aligned with organizational risk decisions instead of being an uncontrolled bridge

00:01:44.397 --> 00:01:46.900
between a model and production systems.
